From b4768b5018baf189905efb51fa6e06c72e2a636d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 15:33:15 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 15/35] vrend: poll an EGL fence without exporting a fence fd

Checking whether a fence has retired must not block: the caller is the
thread that decodes the guest's command stream. A driver that has no
kernel fence object implements EGL_ANDROID_native_fence_sync by waiting
for the fence before it hands back a descriptor, so the poll became an
open-ended wait on a GPU timeline and the guest's next commands were never
decoded.

Poll with eglClientWaitSyncKHR and a zero timeout instead, and keep the
fence-descriptor path for a caller that asked to block, where it still
avoids holding Mesa's EGL display lock across the wait.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c b/src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c
index c15f6b6..9f93e77 100644
--- a/src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c
+++ b/src/vrend/vrend_winsys_egl.c
@@ -952,6 +952,14 @@ static bool client_wait_fence(struct virgl_egl *egl, EGLSyncKHR fence, bool bloc
 bool virgl_egl_client_wait_fence(struct virgl_egl *egl, EGLSyncKHR fence, bool blocking)
 {
 #ifndef _WIN32
+   /* A driver with no kernel fence of its own implements the native fence fd
+    * by waiting for the fence itself, which would turn a poll into a wait on
+    * the thread that has to keep feeding the GPU. Only a caller that means to
+    * block can afford that.
+    */
+   if (!blocking)
+      return client_wait_fence(egl, fence, false);
+
    /* attempt to poll the native fence fd instead of eglClientWaitSyncKHR() to
     * avoid Mesa's eglapi global-display-lock synchronizing vrend's sync_thread.
     */
